# Hard Constraints (always)

These rules override convenience habits. If anything else conflicts, follow this file.

## 1) Commit preparation (mandatory gate)

When the user asks to prepare a commit (e.g. 准备commit, 准备提交, 帮我准备commit, **我要commit**, 写提交信息, commit message, summarize for commit):

- **Prepare ≠ execute:** phrases like 准备/我要 commit mean **draft message + wait for approval**, not immediate `git commit`. See `git-commit-mentor` skill (intent recognition).
- Inspect staged/unstaged changes first; draft from **actual** diffs only.
- Use **Conventional Commits** for the subject: `type(scope): summary` (e.g. `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`). **Summary = concrete work**, not Feature/Slice/Bug IDs (`TEST-F01-S02`); link IDs in docs/PR, optional `Refs:` footnote only (`git-commit-mentor`).
- Run **Doc DoD** checks per `git-commit-mentor` when the change is feature/design/bug-driven; surface gaps with the draft.
- Present the **full draft** to the user and **wait for explicit approval** before `git add` / `git commit`.
- **NEVER** run `git commit` (or push) unless the user clearly approves that draft **and** asks to execute (e.g. 用这个提交、执行 commit) in the same task.
- If the user only wanted a message draft, stop after the draft unless they then ask to commit.

## 2) C/C++ implementation style

When writing or editing `.c`, `.cpp`, `.h`, `.hpp`, `.cc`, `.cxx`:

- Follow the user **cpp-style** skill (and project `.clang-format` / local docs when stricter).
- **Avoid** anonymous-namespace free functions and file-scope `static` free functions when a **class member** (instance or `static` member) is a natural fit.
- **Exceptions** (free functions OK): enum/enum-class helpers, factories/casts, algorithms shared across unrelated types, or cases where a wrapper class is more redundant than one helper.
- If you **must** use anonymous-namespace or file-scope `static` helpers outside those exceptions, **tell the user explicitly** why and what blocked a member function.

## 3) Context recovery (re-apply this rule)

**Re-read and silently re-apply sections 1–5** at the start of your reply when any of these is true:

- New Cursor session or thread, or the conversation was **summarized / compressed** (long history truncated, “continue previous task”, or prior rules seem forgotten).
- User says **re-bootstrap**, **对齐约束**, or asks to realign with project rules.
- You are resuming after a **long tool-heavy stretch** (many reads/edits/builds) and about to make a **non-trivial code change** or **commit-related** action.
- You are unsure whether commit-approval, C++ organization, comment hygiene, or branch naming rules still apply.

After recovery: do not repeat the full rule text to the user unless they ask; just behave according to it.

## 4) Comment hygiene (IDs stay out of code comments)

- **Forbidden** in source comments / code-adjacent inline docs: Feature, Slice, Bug, or ADR IDs (e.g. `CORE-F01`, `CORE-F01-S02`, `BUG-CORE-001`, `ADR-20261015-01`).
- IDs belong in collaboration docs (registry, ACTIVE_WORK, design/plan/progress) and may appear in commit messages when the team uses them there.
- Comments explain non-obvious *why*, invariants, hazards, or trade-offs; keep them short; do not restate the next line of code.

## 5) Branch naming (topic granularity; no registry IDs)

- Feature branches use topic paths: `feat/<topic>` or `feat/<topic>/<subtopic>` (e.g. `feat/render`, `feat/render/material`, `feat/gameplay`).
- Name the **area of change**, not a Feature/Slice row. Prefer an existing matching area branch over a new ID-tied branch.
- **Forbidden:** branch names built around registry IDs (e.g. `feat/core-f01-xxx`, `feat/TMPL-F05`, `feat/core-f01-s01`).
- Same ID ban applies to `fix/` / `refactor/` / `chore/` (and similar): topic words only.
- Profile cannot disable §4 or §5.
